 First, we note that for each element \( k \) in the set \(\{1, 2, \ldots, 12\}\), either \( k \in \mathcal{A} \) or \( k \in \mathcal{B} \), but not both since \(\mathcal{A} \cap \mathcal{B} = \emptyset\). Let \( n = |\mathcal{A}| \) and \( m = |\mathcal{B}| \). Since \(\mathcal{A} \cup \mathcal{B} = \{1, 2, \ldots, 12\}\), we have \( n + m = 12 \). The conditions that \( n \notin \mathcal{A} \) and \( m \notin \mathcal{B} \) imply that \( n \neq |\{1, 2, \ldots, 12\} \setminus \mathcal{A}| \) and \( m \neq |\{1, 2, \ldots, 12\} \setminus \mathcal{B}| \). Therefore, \( n \neq 12 - n \) and \( m \neq 12 - m \), which simplifies to \( n \neq 6 \). Similarly, \( m \neq 6 \). The possible values for \( n \) (and \( m \)) are \( 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 7, 8, 9, 10, 11, \) and \( 12 \). For each feasible \( n \), we can choose \( n \) elements for \(\mathcal{A}\) out of the remaining \( 12 - n \) elements, and the rest will be in \(\mathcal{B}\). The number of ways to do this is \( \binom{12 - n}{n} \). We need to sum over all valid \( n \): \[ N = \sum_{\text{feasible } n} \binom{12 - n}{n} \]

Answer: The number of such ordered pairs is \( \boxed{1560} \)
